.git
.gitignore
.env
.env.*
**/.env
**/.env.*
.github
.vscode
.idea
.agents/
.claude/
.codex/
.qoder/
.workbuddy/
*.md
docs/
node_modules/
frontend/node_modules/
frontend/.next/
frontend/out/
backend/.env
backend/__pycache__/
backend/**/__pycache__/
backend/**/*.pyc
backend/logs/
# Docker matches each pattern per path segment, so the `backend/*.db` and
# `backend/*.json` lines below stop at `backend/` and do not reach the archives
# under here: 30.69 MB across 11 files entered the build context, including two
# 11.9 MB v2_loop.db copies and three event_store.json snapshots from the June
# and July purges. `COPY backend/ /app/` would bake old production records into
# an image layer. Asserted by backend/tests/test_deploy_units.py.
backend/backups/
backend/*.db
backend/*.db-wal
backend/*.db-shm
backend/*.db.bak*
backend/*.db-*
backend/*.sqlite
backend/*.sqlite3
backend/*.json
backend/*.jsonl
# Re-included after the rule above, which is what Docker resolves by: this file
# is tracked, ships 35 warm entries, and is read on every resolve_team_id call.
# resolve_team_id tries the cache, then API-Football, and only then the curated
# 55-entry map -- so losing the file does not fall back for free, it pays a live
# lookup first on every container start. Asserted by test_deploy_units.py.
!backend/world_cup_team_ids.json
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/
.coverage
htmlcov/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
code-review-*/
